============= ex01 ==============
Exercise 01
print_groups.sh
Turn-in directory: ex01/
Files to turn in: print_groups.sh
Allowed functions: None
Write a command line that displays the list of groups the user (defined in the
environment variable FT_USER) belongs to.
• The output should be comma-separated, without spaces.
• Examples:
◦ for FT_USER=bocal:
  $>./print_groups.sh
  bocal,adm,cdrom,sudo,dip,plugdev,lxd,lpadmin,libvirt$>
◦ for FT_USER=daemon:
  $>./print_groups.sh
  daemon,bin$>
Tips:
man id

**********************

So I did the homework, understand the followings:
id -G: print all groups IDs
   -g: print only effective group ID
   -n: print names instead of number
echo $variable: expand (read) the variable and then print it

then, with command tr ' ' ',' we can remove the spaces and let the output be comma-separated
I tried with:
  echo "id -Gn $FT_USER | tr ' ' ','" > print_groups.sh
wrong, because the shell substitutes variables right away. 
So if FT_USER happens to be set to bocal on your machine right now, your script would end up literally containing:
  id -Gn bocal | tr ' ' ','
— hardcoded to bocal forever, regardless of what FT_USER is set to when the grader runs your script later. 
That defeats the whole purpose of using an environment variable.

so
id -Gn $FT_USER
echo 'id -Gn $FT_USER | tr " " ","' > print_groups.sh

then check with:
cat print_groups.sh

turns out:
id -Gn $FT_USER | tr " " ","

I wanted to test with daemon, but it comes out:
  c2r8s5% echo 'id -Gn $FT_USER | tr " " ","' > print_groups.sh
  c2r8s5% cat print_groups.sh
  id -Gn $FT_USER | tr " " ","
  c2r8s5% FT_USER=aemon ./print_groups.sh | cat -e
  zsh: permission denied: ./print_groups.sh

Denied because we don't really have the access, so let's check the permissions first:
  ls -l print_groups.sh
Here I found out that indeed this script has no x anywhere, so I need to assign the execute access to all groups:
  chmod +x print_groups.sh
And test it:
  ls -l print_groups.sh
  -rwxr-xr-x 1 myusername 2026_madrid 29 Aug 11 14:06 print_groups.sh

Try the task again:
  FT_USER=myusername ./print_groups.sh | cat -e


Oops, I found out that I created a print_groups.sh outside of the folder (pulled repository folder), so now there is a separate file print_groups.sh and a folder called C_Shell_01_tasks.
And inside C_Shell_01_tasks, there are nothing.
So I need to create a folder named ex01 inside C_Shell_01_tasks, and then move the print_groups.sh to the ex01 folder.

mkdir -p C_Shell_01_tasks/ex01 
here with the -p, Linux creates any missing parent folders automatically along the path:
  # WITH -p:
  mkdir -p C_Shell_01_tasks/ex01
  # ✅ Works! Creates "C_Shell_01_tasks" if missing, AND creates "ex01" inside it.

  # WITHOUT -p (If "ex01" folder doesn't exist yet):
  mkdir C_Shell_01_tasks/ex01
  # ❌ Error: No such file or directory

mv print_groups.sh C_Shell_01_tasks/ex01/

then can check it with cd and ls

Learnt from Shell 00 ex05 that for script file, better to always include #!/bin/sh as it's recommended by 42 standard
So we may do the following:
touch print_groups.sh
vim print_groups.sh
#!/bin/sh
  #### in vim: 
      press i to enter insert mode
      will see -- INSERT -- at the bottom
      press esc
      type :wq and press Enter
          : opens the command line at the bottom
          w: write
          q: quit
More details:
  tr ' ' ',' can be seen as tr SET1 SET2
  tr: translate
  'SET1': what we are looking for, the original
  'SET2': what we want to change to, the result

  if we do it like: id -Gn FT_USER
  Shell will see it as usual letters FT_USER, look for the user who called FT_USER

  the symbol $ here is the variable
  example:
    FT_USER=daemon (attention, no space before or after =)
    echo FT_USER
    the output will be: FR_USER
    but if we write it as:
    echo $FT_USER
    the output will be: daemon

About variable
  the structure FT_USER=daemon, Shell will see it as NAME=value, so it's variablename=value to be insert in the variable

So finally with ex01:
#!/bin/sh
id -Gn $FT_USER | tr ' ' ','

then when we evaluate, we can check it like this:
FT_USER=bocal
sh print_groups.sh
it shouls output 
  bocal,adm,cdrom,sudo,dip,plugdev,lxd,lpadmin,libvirt
or with | cat -e
  bocal,adm,cdrom,sudo,dip,plugdev,lxd,lpadmin,libvirt$

if we try FT_USER=daemon
sh print_groups.sh
since in the 42 computer daemon only has daemon, output "daemon" as well

==================== ex02 ===================
find_sh.sh
Turn-in directory: ex02/
Files to turn in: find_sh.sh
Allowed functions: None

Write a command line that searches for all files ending with .sh in the current
directory and all subdirectories.
• The output should display only the file names without the .sh extension.
• Example output:
$>./find_sh.sh | cat -e
find_sh$
file1$
file2$
file3$
$>

******************

find [WHERE] [CONDITION]
current directory is .
so find all files in current directory and all subdirectories means that we need to 
find .

basename NAME [SUFFIX]
basename OPTION... NAME...

if we write it this way:
find . -name '*.sh'
    #don't use *.sh directly, because in this way, Shell will expand it first, before the find command
    #for example, if in the folder we have find_sh.sh and hello.sh, if we use *.sh directly, Shell may expand both .sh files before executing find command.
    #so, since we want to pass the complete pattern to the find command, we use '*.sh' instead of *.sh
    #there are more tests of find family let's see it this way
      -name: find by name
      -type: find by type
      -size: find by size
      -user: find by owner
we can also understand the command find as:
find [WHERE] [CONDITION] [WHAT IS THE CONDITION]
    #for example
    find . -name '*.sh'
      #where: .
          it means from the current directory and on, recursive search
      #condition: name
      #what is the condition: *.sh

find [where] [condition] -exec COMMAND {} \;
    # -exec: execute a command to the found result
    # COMMAND: what we want to execute, for example basename
    # {}: the result found
    # \; : tell find: the -exec command ends here


a little trick:
  find . -name '*.sh'
      #do not search files whose name ends in .sh
      #but to search entries whose name matches *.sh
      #because -name only check name
      #so if there is a directory named hello.sh/ , it will be found as well
      #if we only want regular files, then need to add another test (here it means conditions maybe)
      #-type f

basename won't work like tr, what basename needs is argument
|: passes standard output (stdin/stdout), pass stdout to a command's stdin
-exec: use the result of find to execute another command 

so to combine all things up:
  find . -name '*.sh' -exec [COMMAND] [CURRENT RESULT] [REMOVE SUFFIX] \;
    # now we can replace them with [COMMAND] - basename, [CURRENT RESULT] - {}, [REMOVE SUFFIX] - .sh
  find . -name '*.sh' -exec basename {} .sh \;
    # when we test this in terminal, we get:
        find_sh
        hello
        world
      so it didn't show the .sh file inside a directory folder

Since . means current directory, when we do ./find_sh.sh | cat -e , we are asking Shell to run find_sh.sh in the current directory.
with find_sh.sh | cat -e, Shell will go to PATH to look for it
echo $PATH , normally include like /usr/local/bin:/usr/locl/bin:/bin:...
so Shell will look the find_sh.sh inside of:
  /usr/locl/bin/find_sh.sh
  /usr/bin/find_sh.sh
  /bin/find_sh.sh
and they don't exist
will come back to me as: command not found: find_sh.sh

attention, with ./fin_sh.sh | cat -e , we also need to give it permission of execution
chmod +x find_sh.sh
if not, with ./fin_sh.sh | cat -e , it will be deneied as well

we can also use sh find_sh.sh | cat -e
since with sh, actually sh is the command, sh will read the find_sh.sh, and find_sh.sh just need to be read

=========== ex03 =============

count_files.sh
Turn-in directory: ex03/
Files to turn in: count_files.sh
Allowed functions: None
• Write a command line that counts and displays the total number of regular files
and directories in the current directory and all its subdirectories.
• The count should include "." (the starting directory).
• Example output:
$>./count_files.sh | cat -e
42$
$>

********************
mkdir ex03 
cd ex03 
touch count_files.sh 
vim count_files.sh 
write inside count_files.sh the content : 
#!/bin/sh then 
:wq to save and exit

untill now everything fine
now let's figure out the following:
1. find total number of regular files and directories in the current directory and all its subdirectories
2. display them
3. count them
4. the count should include "." (the starting directory)
5. check it with the output example

1. we can do it with find .
if we try find . now we will get:
. 
./count_files.sh #which is the file we just created and edited
#good to know that . is also here, since it's the requirement point 4

2. remember the command wc? for count, we used it to count the bytes, wc -c
wc: word count
it can also count lines, words, bytes/characters
  # -c, bytes
  # -l, lines
  # -m, character
  # -w, words
  # -L, maximum display width
so we want to count the total number of regular files and directories (including subdirectories)
when we used find .
the results are in line, each directory and file is placed in a line
but we only want regular files and directories, not other types
find -type
    # f: regular file
    # d: directory

I tried:

find . -type f&d -exec wc -l
get the following:
./count_files.sh
[1] 327725
[1]  + done       find . -type f
zsh: command not found: d

normal, because it's wrong
here I tried "&", but in Shell, "&" means run the previous command in the background, so what I tried, for Shell it's like:
find . -type f &
another command:
d -exec wc -l

so I see: [1] 327725, it's a background job
the I see: zsh: command not found: d
because Shell tried a command which is d
sure Shell cant'd find it
meanwhile, we don't need -exec wc -l here, because in ex00, we needed -exec since we need to execute basename command for each result of find
ex03 is different, we only need to count the number, so with | is better
receive the output of left and put it into right

it should be:
find . -type f -o -type d
  # find files and directories, both of them
then with | wc -l
we will get the number

attention:
  here the directory ex03 itself is also included, is the starting directory

I tried to create some more files and directories to check:
mkdir test1
touch test2.txt

then with the command: find . -type f -o -type d | wc -l
output: 
.
./count_files.sh
./test1
./test2.txt

which is correct, because it includes . as well
so we open vim count_files.sh
then write the command in the second line:
  find . -type f -o -type d | wc -l
save and quit

then in the ex03 we can check it with:
./count_files.sh -------- attention, need to run it inside of ex03, it's the current directory
or
sh count_files.sh
